SQL SERVER JOIN NOTES
=====================

A JOIN combines rows from two or more tables based on a related column.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
SAMPLE TABLES
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Customers Table:
  CustomerID | Name
  -----------|---------
  1          | Arjun
  2          | Priya
  3          | Ravi
  4          | Sneha

Orders Table:
  OrderID | CustomerID | Amount
  --------|------------|-------
  101     | 1          | 250
  102     | 1          | 400
  103     | 2          | 150
  104     | 99         | 300   <-- no matching customer


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
1. INNER JOIN
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Meaning: Return only rows that have a match in BOTH tables.

Query:
  SELECT c.Name, o.OrderID, o.Amount
  FROM Customers c
  INNER JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID;

Result:
  Name  | OrderID | Amount
  ------|---------|-------
  Arjun | 101     | 250
  Arjun | 102     | 400
  Priya | 103     | 150

Note: Ravi and Sneha (no orders) and OrderID 104 (no customer) are excluded.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
2. LEFT JOIN
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Meaning: Return ALL rows from the left table.
         If there is no match on the right side, show NULL.

Query:
  SELECT c.Name, o.OrderID, o.Amount
  FROM Customers c
  LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID;

Result:
  Name  | OrderID | Amount
  ------|---------|-------
  Arjun | 101     | 250
  Arjun | 102     | 400
  Priya | 103     | 150
  Ravi  | NULL    | NULL
  Sneha | NULL    | NULL

Note: All customers appear. Ravi and Sneha have no orders so they show NULL.

Tip - Find customers with NO orders at all:
  SELECT c.Name
  FROM Customers c
  LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID
  WHERE o.OrderID IS NULL;
  --> Returns: Ravi, Sneha


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
3. RIGHT JOIN
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Meaning: Return ALL rows from the right table.
         If there is no match on the left side, show NULL.

Query:
  SELECT c.Name, o.OrderID, o.Amount
  FROM Customers c
  RIGHT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID;

Result:
  Name  | OrderID | Amount
  ------|---------|-------
  Arjun | 101     | 250
  Arjun | 102     | 400
  Priya | 103     | 150
  NULL  | 104     | 300

Note: All orders appear. OrderID 104 has no customer, so Name is NULL.

Tip: RIGHT JOIN is rarely used. Most developers just flip the tables
     and use LEFT JOIN instead.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
4. FULL OUTER JOIN
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Meaning: Return ALL rows from BOTH tables.
         Show NULL wherever there is no match on either side.

Query:
  SELECT c.Name, o.OrderID, o.Amount
  FROM Customers c
  FULL OUTER JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID;

Result:
  Name  | OrderID | Amount
  ------|---------|-------
  Arjun | 101     | 250
  Arjun | 102     | 400
  Priya | 103     | 150
  Ravi  | NULL    | NULL
  Sneha | NULL    | NULL
  NULL  | 104     | 300

Note: Nobody gets left out. NULLs appear wherever there is no match.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
5. CROSS JOIN
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Meaning: Return every possible combination of rows from both tables.
         No ON clause is needed.

Query:
  SELECT c.Name, o.OrderID
  FROM Customers c
  CROSS JOIN Orders o;

Result: 4 customers x 4 orders = 16 rows total.

Warning: Use carefully. Large tables will produce massive results very fast.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
6. SELF JOIN
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Meaning: Join a table to itself.
         Useful for hierarchies like employees and their managers.

Employees Table:
  EmployeeID | Name      | ManagerID
  -----------|-----------|----------
  1          | Vikram    | NULL
  2          | Anita     | 1
  3          | Rohit     | 1
  4          | Deepika   | 2

Query:
  SELECT e.Name AS Employee, m.Name AS Manager
  FROM Employees e
  LEFT JOIN Employees m ON e.ManagerID = m.EmployeeID;

Result:
  Employee | Manager
  ---------|--------
  Vikram   | NULL
  Anita    | Vikram
  Rohit    | Vikram
  Deepika  | Anita

Note: Same table, two aliases (e and m) to tell the two copies apart.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
COMMON MISTAKE - LEFT JOIN with WHERE filter
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

Putting a WHERE filter on the right table column accidentally turns
a LEFT JOIN into an INNER JOIN, removing your NULL rows.

WRONG:
  SELECT c.Name, o.Amount
  FROM Customers c
  LEFT JOIN Orders o ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID
  WHERE o.Amount > 100;
  --> Ravi and Sneha disappear because NULL > 100 is false.

CORRECT - move the filter into the ON clause:
  SELECT c.Name, o.Amount
  FROM Customers c
  LEFT JOIN Orders o
    ON c.CustomerID = o.CustomerID
    AND o.Amount > 100;
  --> Ravi and Sneha still appear, with NULL in the Amount column.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
QUICK REFERENCE
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  JOIN Type        What it returns
  ---------------  ---------------------------------------------
  INNER JOIN       Rows that match in BOTH tables
  LEFT JOIN        ALL left rows + matching right rows
  RIGHT JOIN       ALL right rows + matching left rows
  FULL OUTER JOIN  ALL rows from both tables
  CROSS JOIN       Every combination of rows (no ON clause)
  SELF JOIN        Table joined to itself (use two aliases)


-----------------------------------------------------------------------
VISUAL SUMMARY
-----------------------------------------------------------------------

  INNER JOIN       LEFT JOIN        RIGHT JOIN       FULL OUTER JOIN
  Only matches     All left         All right        Everything
  [ (match) ]      [LEFT(match) ]   [ (match)RIGHT]  [LEFT(match)RIGHT]
